WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.385 --> 00:00:01.285
- What's up Believe Nation.

00:00:01.285 --> 00:00:04.358
Today I'm going to share with
you my favorite top 10 rules

00:00:04.358 --> 00:00:06.858
to success April 2017 edition.

00:00:08.127 --> 00:00:09.527
And always guys, as people are talking,

00:00:09.527 --> 00:00:11.459
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:11.459 --> 00:00:13.043
please leave it down
in the comments below,

00:00:13.043 --> 00:00:15.572
put quotes around it as well
so other people can be inspired

00:00:15.572 --> 00:00:16.950
and when you're writing something down,

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:21.922
it's much more likely to stick
with yourself as well, enjoy.

00:00:21.922 --> 00:00:24.968
(wind blowing)
(basketball bouncing)

00:00:24.968 --> 00:00:26.974
- The thing is, every
successful person you know

00:00:26.974 --> 00:00:30.474
has failed at what they love to do before.

00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:35.052
I've been unsuccessful in my career,

00:00:35.052 --> 00:00:37.540
you just didn't know about it
'cause I wasn't famous yet,

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.931
I was trying to be famous, I
was trying to get a record deal

00:00:39.931 --> 00:00:43.986
for six years and I kept
getting turned down.

00:00:43.986 --> 00:00:46.147
And any successful person you know,

00:00:46.147 --> 00:00:48.422
even though it seems like
everything just happened for them,

00:00:48.422 --> 00:00:52.018
it usually was preceded
by a bunch of failure.

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:53.510
Or at least a significant amount,

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:56.072
but the great ones aren't
the ones that don't fail,

00:00:56.072 --> 00:00:58.467
they're the ones that
learn from their failure

00:00:58.467 --> 00:00:59.876
and they keep going and they're persistent

00:00:59.876 --> 00:01:02.947
and their tenacious, and for me,

00:01:02.947 --> 00:01:07.114
I was trying to get a
record deal from '98 to 2004

00:01:08.375 --> 00:01:11.638
and I got turned down by
every major record label

00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:14.004
including the one I'm signed to now,

00:01:14.004 --> 00:01:18.134
and for me that means that
even when you hear "no,"

00:01:18.134 --> 00:01:19.671
you have to keep going
through all those nos

00:01:19.671 --> 00:01:21.063
until you get to yes.

00:01:21.063 --> 00:01:23.928
- You know, I grew up in
Colombia, and Colombia

00:01:23.928 --> 00:01:28.414
is a developing country
where a lot of people

00:01:28.414 --> 00:01:31.298
don't have access to the most basic needs

00:01:31.298 --> 00:01:35.242
and where education is considered
a luxury, unfortunately,

00:01:35.242 --> 00:01:39.373
because we all know that
education is a birthright.

00:01:39.373 --> 00:01:43.540
And I just grew up frustrated
seeing how many people

00:01:44.498 --> 00:01:48.665
weren't able to realize
their own dreams, you know?

00:01:50.068 --> 00:01:53.123
When I was eight years
old my dad underwent

00:01:53.123 --> 00:01:55.626
an economic crisis, a financial crisis.

00:01:55.626 --> 00:01:58.986
He lost his business and
we struggled economically

00:01:58.986 --> 00:02:00.712
for many years.

00:02:00.712 --> 00:02:03.962
We lost our car, we lost our furniture,

00:02:06.385 --> 00:02:09.399
and I remember vividly the
day I walked in my house

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:10.547
and all the furniture was gone

00:02:10.547 --> 00:02:13.340
because my dad had to pay his debts.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.090
And it was quite shocking for me.

00:02:18.058 --> 00:02:19.906
I was eight, seven, eight years old

00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:22.727
and when I saw this I was so frustrated.

00:02:22.727 --> 00:02:25.434
And so upset with my parents
because I couldn't understand

00:02:25.434 --> 00:02:28.277
how they did so poorly in business.

00:02:28.277 --> 00:02:30.462
I'm like, why, why?

00:02:30.462 --> 00:02:33.526
I couldn't get it, and my dad and my mom

00:02:33.526 --> 00:02:37.089
took me to the park where
they showed me other kids

00:02:37.089 --> 00:02:39.778
my age sniffing glue, they were orphans,

00:02:39.778 --> 00:02:42.630
they had no one to watch after them

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:46.493
and they were barefoot
and they were abandoned

00:02:46.493 --> 00:02:47.993
to their own luck.

00:02:49.898 --> 00:02:52.232
It probably left such an impression

00:02:52.232 --> 00:02:55.640
in my impressionable mind at that age

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:59.354
that I remember that day and I remember

00:02:59.354 --> 00:03:03.133
I made myself a promise, I said to myself,

00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:07.300
"Shakira, you have to
succeed, because you have to

00:03:08.949 --> 00:03:11.366
"ride in a car again one day.

00:03:12.529 --> 00:03:15.182
"You have to get your own car someday."

00:03:15.182 --> 00:03:18.716
And I wanted to vindicate my parents,

00:03:18.716 --> 00:03:22.199
socially and economically,
and I also wanted to someday

00:03:22.199 --> 00:03:25.556
do something for those kids
who I saw that day in the park,

00:03:25.556 --> 00:03:30.360
so as soon as I had my first
big success with Pies Descalzos

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.635
which was my third album,
I decided to establish

00:03:33.635 --> 00:03:38.280
my own foundation and do my
own contribution to the change,

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.503
even if it was going to be a small one,

00:03:40.503 --> 00:03:44.418
but I wanted to be active and
that's how it all started.

00:03:44.418 --> 00:03:47.752
Going through my own financial struggle

00:03:47.752 --> 00:03:50.218
and the struggle of my
family and understanding

00:03:50.218 --> 00:03:53.298
that the one thing that I never lacked

00:03:53.298 --> 00:03:56.548
was care, protection, and an education,

00:03:57.852 --> 00:04:00.968
and that's probably what
made me thrive in life.

00:04:00.968 --> 00:04:03.801
- Don't wait, go, do it, act, now.

00:04:06.502 --> 00:04:09.303
And really accepting we need
you, we need you, we need you.

00:04:09.303 --> 00:04:12.412
You are the one percent,
you're the percent that says,

00:04:12.412 --> 00:04:14.515
"I'm going to wake up,"
you're the lightworkers.

00:04:14.515 --> 00:04:17.060
You're the ones that
have accepted this call

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.857
in this lifetime at this time
you have decided to wake up

00:04:19.857 --> 00:04:22.774
and chosen to say, "Yes, I'm
here to be of greater service

00:04:22.774 --> 00:04:24.957
"than just to feed myself," right?

00:04:24.957 --> 00:04:26.836
I'm here to feed the world,
I'm here to give love,

00:04:26.836 --> 00:04:28.736
I'm here to be an
expression of that truth.

00:04:28.736 --> 00:04:30.486
Don't hold that back.

00:04:33.074 --> 00:04:34.491
Don't play small.

00:04:35.643 --> 00:04:38.037
We can't, we need you.

00:04:38.037 --> 00:04:40.449
And so really, if you
are anyone in this room

00:04:40.449 --> 00:04:43.699
who has been sitting on that book idea,

00:04:44.651 --> 00:04:45.874
waiting a little bit longer

00:04:45.874 --> 00:04:48.507
before you start that teacher training,

00:04:48.507 --> 00:04:52.674
or doing 10 more teacher trainings
before you actually teach

00:04:54.417 --> 00:04:58.584
if that's you, guys, start
and the pressure will be off.

00:04:59.677 --> 00:05:02.495
I can't begin to tell you, I
could cry just saying this,

00:05:02.495 --> 00:05:06.392
the day I taught Ego
Eradicator I was two weeks

00:05:06.392 --> 00:05:09.926
into my teacher training and
I said, "F it, let's go!"

00:05:09.926 --> 00:05:13.180
And I just went and I
felt so much pressure off.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:16.588
I felt so much lift, because I just knew,

00:05:16.588 --> 00:05:18.556
I felt this technology must be shared.

00:05:18.556 --> 00:05:21.123
I can't hold it, I can't
keep it for myself.

00:05:21.123 --> 00:05:23.192
I can't just sit in my
apartment by myself like this.

00:05:23.192 --> 00:05:27.435
I got to give this.
(audience laughing)

00:05:27.435 --> 00:05:30.251
And sharing it, the pressure
that was just lifted,

00:05:30.251 --> 00:05:33.013
it was like, ah, thank you, thank you.

00:05:33.013 --> 00:05:35.161
And I wasn't the perfect teacher,

00:05:35.161 --> 00:05:37.470
and I looked back and I was
teaching Ego Eradicator wrong,

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:38.850
everyone's arms were like this,

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:40.780
everyone was breathing
backwards, it was a mess,

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:44.007
but I did it and I started,
and in those moments

00:05:44.007 --> 00:05:47.228
when we just get to go, just go,

00:05:47.228 --> 00:05:49.129
the pressure's off and
then we clear the path

00:05:49.129 --> 00:05:53.123
and we are guided to the
next right action, and that

00:05:53.123 --> 00:05:55.592
is the most magnificent
thing we could do right now.

00:05:55.592 --> 00:06:00.560
- Your habits determine
what direction you're going.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:04.076
Your success in life, your results in life

00:06:04.076 --> 00:06:08.206
are in direct proportion to your habits.

00:06:08.206 --> 00:06:11.149
Not what you say you're going to do,

00:06:11.149 --> 00:06:13.178
but what are you going to do?

00:06:13.178 --> 00:06:16.031
You've got to develop successful habits,

00:06:16.031 --> 00:06:20.425
and I believe that starts
with being a daily goal setter

00:06:20.425 --> 00:06:23.685
and a daily goal hitter,
that you get up, you know,

00:06:23.685 --> 00:06:26.562
too many people spend
their whole life, they say

00:06:26.562 --> 00:06:29.900
they're dreaming but they're
really just pipe dreams.

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:32.574
They're thinking about
what they want to be doing

00:06:32.574 --> 00:06:36.084
in 20 years and what their plans are

00:06:36.084 --> 00:06:38.024
way out in the future,
let me tell you what,

00:06:38.024 --> 00:06:40.989
the future you need to be worried about

00:06:40.989 --> 00:06:44.322
is not the next 20 years,
but the next 20 minutes.

00:06:44.322 --> 00:06:46.036
What are you going to be doing?

00:06:46.036 --> 00:06:48.321
What are you going to be
doing to be effective?

00:06:48.321 --> 00:06:51.330
When you get up every
day, know your priorities.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:56.218
Life will overwhelm you,
stuff will overwhelm you.

00:06:56.218 --> 00:06:58.957
With all the information we have now,

00:06:58.957 --> 00:07:01.265
whether it's your phone,
where sometimes I think

00:07:01.265 --> 00:07:03.924
we have smart phones and stupid people.

00:07:03.924 --> 00:07:07.007
I mean, it is so easy to get consumed

00:07:08.137 --> 00:07:11.640
by a bunch of stuff that
is not going to get you

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.594
where you want to get to.

00:07:13.594 --> 00:07:17.886
Say every day, "I will do
today what other's don't

00:07:17.886 --> 00:07:21.438
"so I will have tomorrow
what other's won't."

00:07:21.438 --> 00:07:23.920
You've got to choose to be effective.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:26.260
Know what your priorities are.

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:28.477
Know what your must-dos are.

00:07:28.477 --> 00:07:32.144
Don't let your phone,
don't let your tablet,

00:07:33.406 --> 00:07:36.560
don't let the TV, don't let your friends

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:40.956
knock you off what are
the most important things

00:07:40.956 --> 00:07:42.641
to get done today.

00:07:42.641 --> 00:07:45.444
Sir Winston Churchill
didn't have an inbox,

00:07:45.444 --> 00:07:47.944
he had an action this day box.

00:07:48.943 --> 00:07:52.226
And so things didn't get into that box

00:07:52.226 --> 00:07:54.913
that he wasn't going
to deal with that day.

00:07:54.913 --> 00:07:58.672
As John Wooden said, "Make
today your masterpiece."

00:07:58.672 --> 00:08:02.588
Get up today and get it
done, finish the day,

00:08:02.588 --> 00:08:05.838
attack the day and go change your life.

00:08:06.708 --> 00:08:09.336
- What you represent now,
for all those kids out there

00:08:09.336 --> 00:08:10.643
trying to make it, whatever field it is,

00:08:10.643 --> 00:08:11.951
whether it's music or something else,

00:08:11.951 --> 00:08:14.437
what words do you have for them

00:08:14.437 --> 00:08:15.838
in terms of how you've lived your life,

00:08:15.838 --> 00:08:16.967
and you've made it to this point,

00:08:16.967 --> 00:08:19.060
because you're not supposed
to be here right now.

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:19.893
- Nah, nah.

00:08:19.893 --> 00:08:20.851
- What advice do you have for them

00:08:20.851 --> 00:08:24.189
in terms of their lives and their journey?

00:08:24.189 --> 00:08:25.772
- Lace up, lace up.

00:08:27.175 --> 00:08:28.300
- What does lace up mean?

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:30.061
Say if it's the casual person

00:08:30.061 --> 00:08:32.251
that might be seeing you for
the first time on this show,

00:08:32.251 --> 00:08:33.584
what is lace up?

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:38.724
- It means what you want it to mean, but,

00:08:38.724 --> 00:08:41.411
to me in a day and age like this,

00:08:41.411 --> 00:08:43.991
it means put both feet
on that (bleep) ground,

00:08:43.991 --> 00:08:46.229
(crowd cheering)
lace up,

00:08:46.229 --> 00:08:49.928
get ready to face the world,
go out there, take it on

00:08:49.928 --> 00:08:53.261
and turn a grain of sand into a diamond,

00:08:55.256 --> 00:08:56.513
you know what I'm saying?
- Yeah.

00:08:56.513 --> 00:08:57.446
(crowd cheering)

00:08:57.446 --> 00:09:00.093
I think they love that
answer right there. (laughs)

00:09:00.093 --> 00:09:01.098
They're definitely laced up.

00:09:01.098 --> 00:09:02.090
- See, that's what it's about, man.

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:04.220
You know, it's about
believing in something,

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:05.175
you know what I'm saying?

00:09:05.175 --> 00:09:08.226
Having faith in something,
man, that's what it is.

00:09:08.226 --> 00:09:12.309
We're not trying to hear
some president in a suit

00:09:13.426 --> 00:09:16.093
who can't even live his own life

00:09:18.272 --> 00:09:20.197
because he's so worried
about public perception.

00:09:20.197 --> 00:09:22.030
We can't relate to that,
you know what I'm saying?

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:23.792
They want to see someone
doing what they're doing,

00:09:23.792 --> 00:09:25.161
living how they're living,

00:09:25.161 --> 00:09:26.702
coming from where they're coming from

00:09:26.702 --> 00:09:28.915
and making something better of themselves

00:09:28.915 --> 00:09:30.702
than just sitting on their
ass, you know what I'm saying?

00:09:30.702 --> 00:09:33.281
That's why they're screaming,

00:09:33.281 --> 00:09:36.516
because they see that and
that's what I'm coming with

00:09:36.516 --> 00:09:38.484
on this album, I'm just going
to be the people's champ.

00:09:38.484 --> 00:09:39.391
- Yeah.
- You know what I mean?

00:09:39.391 --> 00:09:42.730
I don't want to hear some reverend tell me

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:44.506
I have to believe in
an organized religion,

00:09:44.506 --> 00:09:45.878
I don't want to hear
some president tell me

00:09:45.878 --> 00:09:47.007
what he's going to do or not going to do

00:09:47.007 --> 00:09:49.693
because they never do it.

00:09:49.693 --> 00:09:52.096
I don't want to hear
some teacher put a limit

00:09:52.096 --> 00:09:55.096
on where my education could take me,

00:09:56.044 --> 00:09:56.877
you know what I'm saying?

00:09:56.877 --> 00:10:01.044
They want to teach me to go
and fix lights in the house,

00:10:01.884 --> 00:10:04.639
but they don't want to tell me
that I can take my own career

00:10:04.639 --> 00:10:06.703
into my own hands and be an entrepreneur

00:10:06.703 --> 00:10:07.818
and come up with something like this.

00:10:07.818 --> 00:10:10.798
They didn't teach you
that you could do this.

00:10:10.798 --> 00:10:12.960
I'm not scared, man, I'm ready to step up

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:16.554
and be a leader for this generation

00:10:16.554 --> 00:10:18.050
and be a voice for this generation

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:19.926
and all these people can
act like they're talking

00:10:19.926 --> 00:10:22.750
for these kids, but it's just an act.

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:24.443
I'm still one of those kids, man.

00:10:24.443 --> 00:10:26.643
This means the world to me,
and that's what this album

00:10:26.643 --> 00:10:30.810
is about and I just know things
are going to change, man.

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:35.542
I feel it, I believe
in like, I don't know.

00:10:35.542 --> 00:10:39.666
- I think if you believe
in what you're doing,

00:10:39.666 --> 00:10:44.155
many people will tell
you why you're mistaken,

00:10:44.155 --> 00:10:47.832
why it'll never work, but if
you really believe passionately

00:10:47.832 --> 00:10:51.999
what you're doing, and keep
pushing on, keep pushing on

00:10:53.154 --> 00:10:56.921
until you either succeed
or at least you realize

00:10:56.921 --> 00:10:59.588
that it's just not going to happen.

00:11:00.565 --> 00:11:05.243
And then if you don't
succeed, pick yourself up

00:11:05.243 --> 00:11:09.410
and try again and ultimately,
if you're that determined

00:11:13.019 --> 00:11:15.102
you will succeed in life.

00:11:16.135 --> 00:11:20.131
And if you don't you'll have
a hell of a lot of fun trying.

00:11:20.131 --> 00:11:23.929
I'm most comfortable
when I'm in the studio.

00:11:23.929 --> 00:11:25.858
And nothing else matters but the music.

00:11:25.858 --> 00:11:27.410
You got to just love for it,

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:29.633
for something that you think
about, you sleep and you dream

00:11:29.633 --> 00:11:32.797
and you wake up and you breath
it and you want to live it.

00:11:32.797 --> 00:11:35.781
There's so many options,
there's many ways to blow up,

00:11:35.781 --> 00:11:38.228
there's many ways to be
known and to get success

00:11:38.228 --> 00:11:39.907
and to be successful,

00:11:39.907 --> 00:11:42.133
and it's just about picking
that way that fits you.

00:11:42.133 --> 00:11:45.076
- I kept going, I had a path,
I knew what my path was.

00:11:45.076 --> 00:11:48.400
My path was to become what I am right now.

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:51.699
Now, I lost sight of it in
an interesting sort of way

00:11:51.699 --> 00:11:54.782
or I went about it in a wayward path,

00:11:57.364 --> 00:12:01.798
but fortunately for me,
all the gettin' high,

00:12:01.798 --> 00:12:04.711
the drinkin', the drugs,
everything else I did,

00:12:04.711 --> 00:12:06.725
I showed up at the theater every day.

00:12:06.725 --> 00:12:09.148
- Right.
- I did plays

00:12:09.148 --> 00:12:12.973
ripped out of my mind, I
rehearsed ripped out of my mind,

00:12:12.973 --> 00:12:17.613
but every lesson that I learned,
I held onto for some reason

00:12:17.613 --> 00:12:20.512
God, whatever, somebody let me hold on

00:12:20.512 --> 00:12:22.271
to everything that I was learning.

00:12:22.271 --> 00:12:24.102
My stage lefts and my stage rights,

00:12:24.102 --> 00:12:27.850
my upstage foot always
out, turn down this way,

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:30.612
learning how to develop a
character, doing all those things,

00:12:30.612 --> 00:12:32.267
because those were the things that I loved

00:12:32.267 --> 00:12:35.082
and I did them in the
midst of everything else

00:12:35.082 --> 00:12:38.259
that I was doing to when my friends

00:12:38.259 --> 00:12:40.016
were getting ready to go
get some more drugs, I'd go,

00:12:40.016 --> 00:12:41.585
"Oh, no, I got to be at the theater,

00:12:41.585 --> 00:12:44.094
"I'll catch you all when the
theater's over," when I'm done.

00:12:44.094 --> 00:12:47.624
And I'd get, or I got to go
build a set, I got to do this.

00:12:47.624 --> 00:12:49.764
So I did everything that I had to do,

00:12:49.764 --> 00:12:52.702
so I was focused on this
by any means necessary.

00:12:52.702 --> 00:12:54.801
Plus I had a professor who was like,

00:12:54.801 --> 00:12:56.819
"Well, you know, all the
great ones do their jobs

00:12:56.819 --> 00:12:58.459
"and get ripped out of their minds.

00:12:58.459 --> 00:13:01.558
"You know, you look at the
Peter O'Tooles and the,

00:13:01.558 --> 00:13:03.541
"they get drunk and they get
high, they do what they do,

00:13:03.541 --> 00:13:05.509
"but they show up and they do the job."

00:13:05.509 --> 00:13:08.624
So we were like, "OK,"
and that's what we did.

00:13:08.624 --> 00:13:10.371
You know, we drank with him and we went

00:13:10.371 --> 00:13:13.174
to styles of acting class,
we'd go smoke a joint

00:13:13.174 --> 00:13:15.952
and come in and do our
other acting classes

00:13:15.952 --> 00:13:17.638
or go build a set or build some costumes

00:13:17.638 --> 00:13:19.382
or hang some lights and
do all of the things

00:13:19.382 --> 00:13:20.463
that we had to do,

00:13:20.463 --> 00:13:22.909
so I kept learning my
lessons and doin' 'em.

00:13:22.909 --> 00:13:25.935
- Sometimes a lot of people
may try a new initiative

00:13:25.935 --> 00:13:27.877
and then they don't get
an immediate reaction

00:13:27.877 --> 00:13:29.579
and they think it's a failure.

00:13:29.579 --> 00:13:30.412
- [Gary] That's right.

00:13:30.412 --> 00:13:31.755
- I want to go back to your YouTube videos

00:13:31.755 --> 00:13:34.531
with the Wine Library, you
had so much content out there.

00:13:34.531 --> 00:13:35.606
How did you stick in there,

00:13:35.606 --> 00:13:38.614
how did you keep yourself motivated?

00:13:38.614 --> 00:13:39.841
- Because I believe in my strategies

00:13:39.841 --> 00:13:43.341
and I'm patient as, like, all great stuff,

00:13:44.221 --> 00:13:47.084
especially when you're innovating
doesn't happen right away.

00:13:47.084 --> 00:13:49.182
This notion that like,
"Oh Gary, I tried it,

00:13:49.182 --> 00:13:52.115
"I spent 500 bucks on Facebook
ads, it doesn't work."

00:13:52.115 --> 00:13:53.211
Well what if your videos sucked,

00:13:53.211 --> 00:13:56.924
what if your product and service sucks?

00:13:56.924 --> 00:13:58.995
I tend to do things that are very early,

00:13:58.995 --> 00:14:00.355
that has been my career.

00:14:00.355 --> 00:14:03.446
When I do things I believe in them.

00:14:03.446 --> 00:14:06.215
I stick with them based on intuition,

00:14:06.215 --> 00:14:09.510
even though for 18 months
nobody watched my wine videos

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:12.056
at all, I knew it was right, I was right,

00:14:12.056 --> 00:14:13.856
there's been things I've
done that I jumped off of

00:14:13.856 --> 00:14:16.993
very quickly, I started a
Gary V app, VeeChat, V E E,

00:14:16.993 --> 00:14:19.712
like WeChat, I thought
it was funny for my fans,

00:14:19.712 --> 00:14:22.760
great CRM one-on-one, didn't
feel right after a week or two

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:24.921
spent plenty of money building it,

00:14:24.921 --> 00:14:27.504
stopped it after couple months.

00:14:28.443 --> 00:14:31.236
Wine Library TV, five
years, every day YouTube,

00:14:31.236 --> 00:14:34.320
decided to change it to
Daily Grape, insular app,

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:36.260
you could buy stuff,
subscription for reviews,

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:38.507
it was a lot of fun, only
did it for five months,

00:14:38.507 --> 00:14:41.545
stopped it 'cause it
didn't feel right anymore.

00:14:41.545 --> 00:14:44.715
It's always hard to know when
to jump off something new,

00:14:44.715 --> 00:14:48.019
but I find that people don't
believe in what they're doing.

00:14:48.019 --> 00:14:52.261
They hear a guru and
they do it begrudgingly

00:14:52.261 --> 00:14:53.975
'cause their boss wants
them to or they're like,

00:14:53.975 --> 00:14:55.500
well, Gary's right a lot, so let me do it,

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:57.932
but they don't believe
it and so then they jump.

00:14:57.932 --> 00:14:59.909
They're looking for nos instead of yesses.

00:14:59.909 --> 00:15:01.603
Everything I do, every
business development,

00:15:01.603 --> 00:15:04.568
every event I do, every time
I try a new marketing tactic

00:15:04.568 --> 00:15:06.452
or a sales tactic I'm looking for the yes.

00:15:06.452 --> 00:15:07.285
- Right.

00:15:07.285 --> 00:15:10.103
- I'm looking for the yes,
and if the no punches me,

00:15:10.103 --> 00:15:12.353
well then I'm like,
crap, the no punched me.

00:15:12.353 --> 00:15:14.584
I believe the majority of
people when they try new tactics

00:15:14.584 --> 00:15:17.728
are looking for the no, and
you're going to be getting

00:15:17.728 --> 00:15:19.274
a lot of subtle nos immediately.

00:15:19.274 --> 00:15:20.107
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:15:20.107 --> 00:15:21.725
- And so I think that's why that happens.

00:15:21.725 --> 00:15:25.392
- Your success has been
dramatic, obviously,

00:15:26.929 --> 00:15:29.142
and coming from a city
like Chennai, of course,

00:15:29.142 --> 00:15:33.602
culturally very rich, we are
in our own state of Tamil Nadu,

00:15:33.602 --> 00:15:36.432
but more importantly, to be
able to go global with that

00:15:36.432 --> 00:15:39.668
has been just quite
awe-inspiring to be honest

00:15:39.668 --> 00:15:41.845
and made all of us so
proud here in Chennai,

00:15:41.845 --> 00:15:44.534
but if you look at young musicians today

00:15:44.534 --> 00:15:47.671
and what they are being offered,

00:15:47.671 --> 00:15:51.635
and the technology, it's
moved along as well,

00:15:51.635 --> 00:15:52.806
what would you say to them?

00:15:52.806 --> 00:15:55.549
- I think they have to
find their own voice.

00:15:55.549 --> 00:15:57.027
They find their own inner voice.

00:15:57.027 --> 00:15:59.371
The moment we try to do somebody else's,

00:15:59.371 --> 00:16:01.250
we become mediocre.

00:16:01.250 --> 00:16:05.417
The moment we try, our limitations
are sometimes are plusses

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:10.922
so sometimes that forces
you to think in a way where,

00:16:10.922 --> 00:16:13.465
there are many millions
of ways to achieve a song,

00:16:13.465 --> 00:16:17.632
or achieve greatness in art,
so you find a different way.

00:16:20.587 --> 00:16:23.571
You know, a classic
example is Kumar Gandharva,

00:16:23.571 --> 00:16:26.414
a classical singer, he had a lung problem

00:16:26.414 --> 00:16:29.513
or something so he
couldn't sustain his notes.

00:16:29.513 --> 00:16:31.763
If you listen to his rendering,

00:16:31.763 --> 00:16:34.425
he would just make everything short.

00:16:34.425 --> 00:16:36.619
(singing with abrupt end)

00:16:36.619 --> 00:16:38.274
He wouldn't like, (sings drawn-out note)

00:16:38.274 --> 00:16:40.331
he would never sustain it
because of his breathing problem.

00:16:40.331 --> 00:16:42.092
But that became a great style.

00:16:42.092 --> 00:16:43.968
You don't have to copy
that because his limitation

00:16:43.968 --> 00:16:48.053
became a style of what
he could amazingly do

00:16:48.053 --> 00:16:50.993
and it was unique when he sang.

00:16:50.993 --> 00:16:53.645
And so I think all of us have limitations

00:16:53.645 --> 00:16:57.036
and we should not think it's a limitation.

00:16:57.036 --> 00:16:59.404
So there's something else which is there,

00:16:59.404 --> 00:17:02.121
a plus within you and then bring that out

00:17:02.121 --> 00:17:03.554
and then you become totally different.

00:17:03.554 --> 00:17:05.776
- You weren't created by a mediocre God.

00:17:05.776 --> 00:17:07.242
You weren't created to be mediocre,

00:17:07.242 --> 00:17:09.789
you were created for good works
which He planned beforehand.

00:17:09.789 --> 00:17:11.609
You were created to do amazing things,

00:17:11.609 --> 00:17:14.535
and sometimes we just got
to realize our potential.

00:17:14.535 --> 00:17:17.178
We got to realize it, we were
created by an awesome God,

00:17:17.178 --> 00:17:18.390
we were created to do amazing things,

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:20.302
and if sometimes we
just have the confidence

00:17:20.302 --> 00:17:23.162
that we were created to
do something special,

00:17:23.162 --> 00:17:25.700
then go do something special,

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:27.761
but you have to believe in yourself.

00:17:27.761 --> 00:17:29.401
You have to believe that you are gifted

00:17:29.401 --> 00:17:31.093
for such a time as this,
you have to believe

00:17:31.093 --> 00:17:33.053
that you have the talent,
you have to believe

00:17:33.053 --> 00:17:35.127
that you can make the
choice every single day

00:17:35.127 --> 00:17:37.905
to wake up, to love it,
to be passionate about it

00:17:37.905 --> 00:17:39.496
and sacrifice.

00:17:39.496 --> 00:17:40.777
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:17:40.777 --> 00:17:42.154
I'd love to know, what did you think?

00:17:42.154 --> 00:17:43.295
What was your favorite clip?

00:17:43.295 --> 00:17:44.630
What are you going to take from this video

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:47.484
and immediately apply to your
life or your business somehow?

00:17:47.484 --> 00:17:48.787
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:17:48.787 --> 00:17:51.025
I'm super curious to find
out, so thank you guys again

00:17:51.025 --> 00:17:52.815
for watching, I believe in you,

00:17:52.815 --> 00:17:54.959
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:17:54.959 --> 00:17:59.668
and whatever your one word is,
much love, I'll see you soon.

00:17:59.668 --> 00:18:02.348
(wind howling)
(basketball bouncing)

